{
  "meta": {
    "title": "Domoic Acid on the Shelf: Pseudo-nitzschia Across the Bering–Chukchi–Beaufort",
    "date": "2026-07-28",
    "lesson": "Pseudo-nitzschia is a pennate diatom found from the equator to the poles, and only some of its species make the neurotoxin domoic acid. Along the Alaska coast the assemblage turns over as you move north and as the season runs on: smaller, warmer-water species through the Bering Strait and Chukchi in summer, and the large, strongly toxic P. seriata on the Beaufort shelf later in the year. Which species is present therefore decides whether a bloom is food or a poisoning event. Watch what happens here when a chain sinks out of the light. A diatom eats nothing but light, so below the photic zone it is on a clock; when it dies it releases its biomass, which is a windfall for you, and — if it was a toxic species — domoic acid into the water, which kills the grazers that were hunting you. The toxin is not a weapon the alga fires. It is what is left behind when it dies, which is exactly why a harmful bloom is most dangerous as it collapses.",
    "citation": "Hubbard, Villac, Chadwick (2023). Spatiotemporal transitions in Pseudo-nitzschia species assemblages and domoic acid along the Alaska coast. doi:10.1371/journal.pone.0282794",
    "difficulty": "hard",
    "realWorldBasis": "Pseudo-nitzschia assemblages and domoic acid across Bering/Chukchi/Beaufort water masses, 2018 DBO/AON surveys"
  },
